{"name":"Butterfly Blue","hex":"#2099bb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"butterfly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flying insect of the order Lepidoptera, distinguished from moths by their diurnal activity and generally brighter colouring. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\n\n The imago (final form) of such an insect, consisting usually of a long body with large flat wings, as opposed to earlier instars such as caterpillar or pupa.","The imago (final form) of such an insect, consisting usually of a long body with large flat wings, as opposed to earlier instars such as caterpillar or pupa."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cut (food) almost entirely in half and spread the halves apart, in a shape suggesting the wings of a butterfly.","To cut strips of surgical tape or plasters into thin strips, and place across (a gaping wound) to close it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/butterfly"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Butterfly Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Butterfly Blue is the debut studio album by Australian singer-songwriter Mallrat. It was announced on 2 March 2022, alongside single, \"Teeth\". Upon announcement, Mallrat said \"I've always valued music that is interesting, beautiful, and unpretentious. Something timeless and not reactive. Butterfly Blue was made with that in mind. It's a demonstration of not pretending to be anyone else.\""}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/01/Butterfly_Blue_by_Mallrat.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butterfly_Blue","wikidata":"Q116675850"},{"title":"Butterfly","blocks":[{"text":"Butterflies are winged insects from the lepidopteran superfamily Papilionoidea, characterised by large, often brightly coloured wings that often fold together when at rest, and a conspicuous, fluttering flight. The oldest butterfly fossils have been dated to the Paleocene, about 56 million years ago, though molecular evidence suggests that they likely originated in the Cretaceous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Fesoj_-_Papilio_machaon_%28by%29.jpg/330px-Fesoj_-_Papilio_machaon_%28by%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butterfly","wikidata":"Q11946202"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg/500px-Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg/1280px-Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg/500px-Common_brimstone_butterfly_%28Gonepteryx_rhamni%29_male_in_flight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Possibly the original butter-fly.[7] A male brimstone (Gonepteryx rhamni) in flight.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACommon_brimstone_butterfly_(Gonepteryx_rhamni)_male_in_flight.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Prodryas.png/500px-Prodryas.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Prodryas.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Prodryas.png/500px-Prodryas.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProdryas.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/PZSL1889Plate31_Fossil_Papilionid_Butterfly_Lithopsyche_antiqua_from_Early_Oligocene_Bembridge_Marls.png/500px-PZSL1889Plate31_Fossil_Papilionid_Butterfly_Lithopsyche_antiqua_from_Early_Oligocene_Bembridge_Marls.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/PZSL1889Plate31_Fossil_Papilionid_Butterfly_Lithopsyche_antiqua_from_Early_Oligocene_Bembridge_Marls.png/960px-PZSL1889Plate31_Fossil_Papilionid_Butterfly_Lithopsyche_antiqua_from_Early_Oligocene_Bembridge_Mar